build-tool-paths-unquoted — variables holding a program or file path are not quoted
====================================================================================

Reported from: the SCHMS+ CI on a Windows runner (fabrik, project
dmoe-pop/epure/es/schms), building clautolisp from source in-job.
2026-08-06.

Symptom
-------

    $ make build-programs
    /usr/bin/sh: line 1: C:/Program: No such file or directory
    make: *** [Makefile:194: build-programs] Error 127

`sh` split the command on the space in `C:/Program Files/Steel Bank
Common Lisp/sbcl` and went looking for an executable literally named
`C:/Program`. Exit 127 is "command not found", not a build error.

Windows is only where it surfaced first, because every stock installer
puts its tool under `C:\Program Files`. **This is not a Windows issue.**
A POSIX path may contain a space just as well — a checkout under
`~/Mes projets/`, a macOS `/Volumes/Disque externe/`, a CI workspace
named after a branch — and any variable holding a program or a file path
can be set to one by the caller. `SBCL` is not special: it is documented
as overridable, so `make SBCL="/opt/mes outils/sbcl"` is a legitimate
invocation that must work.

The rule is therefore general, and has nothing to do with the platform:
**every variable that holds a program or a file path must be quoted at
every point of use, in Makefiles and in scripts alike.**

Cause
-----

The failure above comes from **`$(MAKE)`**, not from `$(SBCL)`. Line 194
is `build-programs`:

    Makefile:194
        @for l in $(RELEASE_LISPS); do $(MAKE) build-$$l || exit $$?; done

`make` sets `MAKE` to its own invocation path. On the reporting machine
that is `C:\Program Files\make-4.4.1-with-guile-w32\bin\make.exe`, so
`sh` sees `C:/Program` in command position and reports it missing. Note
that `SBCL ?= sbcl` is the default in every sub-Makefile, so `$(SBCL)`
was never the spaced value here — an earlier version of this issue said
otherwise and was wrong.

The same defect nevertheless applies to the tool variables, and will bite
as soon as a caller uses the documented override:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:240
        $(SBCL) --noinform --no-userinit --non-interactive \

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:253
        $(CCL) --no-init --quiet \

With `SBCL` left at its default `sbcl`, found on PATH, this works. With
`make SBCL="/opt/mes outils/sbcl"` it word-splits exactly like `$(MAKE)`
does today.

`$(MAKE)` deserves its own mention because it is set by make itself, not
by the caller: no configuration avoids it, and every recursive recipe is
affected.

Note that the *diagnostics* around those lines already quote correctly:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile:157
        echo "Error: sbcl not found in PATH (looked for '$(SBCL)')"; \

so the fix is to extend to the invocations the care already taken in the
messages.

Extent
------

Two counts, because the honest figure depends on a judgement no script
can make.

The **confirmed lower bound** — recipe lines interpolating a known tool
variable (`SBCL`, `CCL`, `LISP`, `CLAUTOLISP`, `ALFE`, `PYTHON`, `EMACS`,
`PANDOC`) unquoted:

    autolisp-front-end/Makefile   4      autolisp-spec/Makefile    1
    clautolisp/Makefile           4      autolisp-test/Makefile    1
    autolisp-benchmark/Makefile   1      documentation/Makefile    1

The **upper bound** — every recipe line with an unquoted `$(VAR)`
interpolation, all variables, all Makefiles:

    Makefile                     45 / 210 recipe lines
    clautolisp/Makefile          79 / 266
    autolisp-test/Makefile       38 / 111
    autolisp-front-end/Makefile  35 / 173
    autolisp-benchmark/Makefile  34 /  65
    autolisp-spec/Makefile       34 /  78
    documentation/Makefile        3 /   9
    ────────────────────────────────────
    total                       268

Most of those 268 are harmless — `$(MAKE)`, a list in a `for`, a numeric
flag. But the set that *must* be quoted is not a fixed list of names: it
is every variable that can hold a path, which includes `PREFIX`,
`DESTDIR`, output files, source files, and directories passed to
`$(MAKE) -C`. Deciding which is which is a reading task, not a grep.

The three scripts that interpolate the same variables want the same
review:

    scripts/run-pathname-probe.sh
    scripts/run-encoding-experiment.sh
    scripts/verify-bricscad-automation.sh

Worth particular attention, because the failure is silent rather than
loud: `command -v $(SBCL)` in the HAVE_* probes
(autolisp-front-end/Makefile:58-59). With a spaced path it does not
error — it answers about the wrong thing, and the build proceeds on a
false premise.

Requested
---------

Quote **every** variable holding a program or a file path, at every point
of use, in Makefiles and in scripts: `"$(SBCL)"`, `"$(CCL)"`,
`"$(CLAUTOLISP)"`, `"$(ALFE)"`, `"$(PREFIX)"`, `"$(DESTDIR)"`, target and
prerequisite paths, directories given to `$(MAKE) -C`. The `command -v`
probes too.

This is a correctness rule, not a portability accommodation: it makes the
documented overrides actually usable. A user who sets
`make SBCL="/opt/mes outils/sbcl"` is doing nothing wrong.

Note that the diagnostics already quote correctly — for instance
autolisp-front-end/Makefile:157, `looked for '$(SBCL)'`. The fix extends
to the invocations the care already taken in the messages.

Worth doing rather than working around. The caller-side workaround is to
set `SBCL=sbcl` and rely on PATH, which works, but it hides the defect
and it takes away an override the build documents as available.

Suggested test
--------------

A build with a tool path containing a space — on Linux, no Windows
needed, which is the point:

    mkdir -p "/tmp/faux outils"
    ln -s "$(command -v sbcl)" "/tmp/faux outils/sbcl"
    make build-programs SBCL="/tmp/faux outils/sbcl"

Worth extending to a checkout whose own path contains a space, which
exercises the target and prerequisite side rather than only the tool
side.

Why it matters
--------------

It is what currently stops `build:clautolisp:windows` from succeeding in
the SCHMS+ pipeline, and with it the whole Windows test lane — the CAD
tests included, since they need alfe on the machine that has the CAD.

The Linux lane is unaffected *by chance*: `sbcl` happens to be on PATH
there under a path without spaces. Move the CI workspace under a
directory with a space, or let anyone use the documented `SBCL=` override
with such a path, and the same failure appears on Linux.


RÉSOLU 2026-08-10 (clautolisp 1.8.27)
=====================================

105 sites quotés dans les sept Makefiles, plus les quatre sondes
`command -v`. Portée : toute interpolation en ligne de recette des
variables d'outil (SBCL, CCL, LISP, CLAUTOLISP, ALFE, PYTHON, EMACS,
PANDOC) **et de $(MAKE)**, qui est la cause réelle du rapport d'origine
(`C:/Program Files/make-4.4.1-with-guile-w32/bin/make.exe`).

    Makefile                     54    autolisp-test/Makefile       9
    clautolisp/Makefile          16    autolisp-spec/Makefile       7
    autolisp-front-end/Makefile  14    autolisp-benchmark/Makefile  5

Les occurrences déjà protégées ont été laissées telles quelles : le
correctif saute celles qui sont directement entre guillemets et celles
qui se trouvent à l'intérieur d'une chaîne déjà ouverte (les
diagnostics du type `looked for '$(SBCL)'`, que le ticket signalait
comme déjà corrects).

Les trois scripts nommés par le ticket — run-pathname-probe.sh,
run-encoding-experiment.sh, verify-bricscad-automation.sh — se sont
avérés DÉJÀ propres à la relecture : la seule interpolation non nue est
dans une chaîne diagnostique déjà entre guillemets. Aucune modification.

Preuve avant/après, avec un outil sous un chemin espacé
(`/…/mes outils/sbcl`) :

    avant :  HAVE_SBCL :=            <- vide
    après :  HAVE_SBCL := yes

C'est exactement le mode d'échec silencieux que le ticket décrivait pour
`command -v` : pas d'erreur, une mauvaise réponse, et la build continue
sur une fausse prémisse. Suite complète après correction : 6321 checks,
0 échec.

NON FAIT, délibérément : la « borne haute » de 268 interpolations. Le
ticket la qualifie lui-même de « reading task, not a grep », et la
majorité est inoffensive ($(MAKE) en position d'argument, listes de
`for`, drapeaux numériques). Quoter PREFIX/DESTDIR au cas par cas
demande de distinguer chemin et liste, ce qui mérite sa propre passe
relue plutôt qu'une réécriture automatique de masse.
